    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-061: Layout perf diagnostic — typed-field hypothesis disproven**. **Decision record, zero code changes.**\n\nRecords the post-ADR-060 re-profile that disproved the typed-`ComputedStyle`-fields hypothesis and closes the perf stream.\n\n## The disproved hypothesis\n\n**ADR-061 plan:** typed `ComputedStyle` fields (e.g. `float margin_top`) alongside the map, eliminating `unordered_map::find`. Projected **~25% layout win** based on ADR-060's pre-fix profile.\n\n**Measured target:** the exact lookup typed fields would eliminate is **0.25% self-time, not 25%.**\n\n**Why the projection was wrong:** ADR-060's copy elimination already captured ~99% of the map-lookup cost by removing the string allocations around it. The map itself was never the bottleneck.\n\n## Post-ADR-060 profile (1135 samples)\n\n| Symbol | Self % | Category |\n|---|---|---|\n| `contains_word` | 5.43% | Cascade `[class~=]` matching |\n| `do_lookup_x` | 1.79% | Linker (first-load, ignore) |\n| `matches_simple` / `SelectorList::matches` / `matches_from` | 2.7% combined | Cascade |\n| `_Hashtable<string,string>::find` | ~1.2% nested | `Stylesheet::variables` — **cascade**, not layout |\n| `_Hashtable<string,CascadeValue>::find` | **0.18–0.26%** | **Typed-fields target** |\n\n## Three candidate targets individually disproven\n\n1. **Typed `ComputedStyle` fields** — 0.25%, not 25%. ADR-060 already captured the cost.\n2. **String→string map** — traced to `Stylesheet::variables` (cascade-side). After ADR-059 cascade is ~7% of total; even perfect fix is ~1%.\n3. **`parse_length` fast path** — not in top-10 self-time post-ADR-060.\n\n## `contains_word` documented as real but small\n\n5.43% self-time is the largest remaining. Maximum possible win is 3-5% of total. **Deferred to a bundled cascade-perf round** (which would touch selector matching comprehensively), not shipped as a standalone polish ADR.\n\n## The perf stream conclusion\n\n| ADR | Cycle | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| 058 | Split-timer infrastructure | Measured cascade 1058ms / layout 2379ms (no fix, just measurement) |\n| 059 | Cascade index | **5.6× cascade win** (3-run stable) |\n| 060 | Copy elimination | **12% layout win** (median-of-3) |\n| **061** | **Diagnostic (typed fields)** | **Hypothesis disproven, 0.25% target not 25%** |\n\n**Combined ADR-059+060: 25.5% total Wikipedia render improvement** (3437ms → ~2555ms).\n\n## Why record a disproven hypothesis as a full ADR\n\n**Disproven hypotheses are valuable.** Without this re-profile, a future contributor reading ADR-060 would reasonably propose typed fields as the next cycle. ADR-061 short-circuits that mistake.\n\nThe cost of ADR-061 (one docs commit) is dwarfed by the cost of a future contributor starting a typed-fields cycle based on stale numbers.\n\n## Why \"flat profile\" is a success signal\n\nWhen no function exceeds ~5% self-time and the top-10 covers ~15% total, **the profile is flat.** That's the correct shape *after* a successful optimization cycle. The progression from \"25.5% total win in 2 cycles\" to \"3-5% for one cycle\" is not a decline — **it's convergence to the floor.**\n\n## The methodology validated\n\nThe three cycles (059, 060, 061) demonstrate all three outcomes:\n- **Measured win** (059: 5.6× cascade)\n- **Measured win** (060: 12% layout)\n- **Measured disproval** (061: typed fields disproven)\n\n**No cycle optimized without measuring.** That's what kept the ledger honest through every step.\n\n## Future triggers for revisiting\n\n1. Adding a feature (JS, images, grid) that shifts the profile\n2. A user-visible perf regression pointing back to layout\n3. Implementation of an incremental-restyle path (typed fields would be more valuable for diffing)\n4. A new architecture (multi-process, JIT) where flat data structures matter\n\n## What this PR carries\n\n- ADR-061 spec + Status: Implemented (decision record, no code)\n- README row 061 added\n- CHANGELOG entry with full profile data + methodology record\n\n## Test count\n\n- Unchanged at 683 / 2614\n- Documentation only; zero code changes\n\nRefs #263\n",
